      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-184089",
      ":question/problem": "Suppose we wish to construct a direct proof of the following statement:\n\n> If $n$ is an integer, then $n^{2} + 7n + 4$ is even.\n\nA proof schema is outlined below. Fill in the missing entries.\n\nLet $n$ be an integer. We consider the following cases:\n\n**Case 1**. If $n$ is even, then $n =$ {{field-1}} for some integer $a$.\n\nBy substituting the above into {{field-2}}, we can show that\n$2a$ {{field-3}}\nwhere $b$ is {{field-4}}.\n\nSince $n^{2} + 7n + 4$ {{field-5}} a multiple of $2$, we conclude that $n^{2} + 7n + 4$ is {{field-6}}.\n\n**Case 2**. If $n$ is odd, then $n =$ {{field-7}} for some integer $a$.\n\nBy substituting the above into {{field-8}}, we can show that\n$2b$ {{field-9}}\nwhere $b$ is {{field-10}}.\n\nSince $n^{2} + 7n + 4$ {{field-11}} a multiple of $2$, we conclude that $n^{2} + 7n + 4$ is {{field-12}}.\n\nTherefore, combining the cases above, we conclude that $n^{2} + 7n + 4$ is {{field-13}} for any integer $n$."
